{"kind":"assert","text":"CDF-1 (27cb5f60) is built at 5339ccc5 on builder/cdf1-scope-bytes-cap-20261113 (on main 324a0679, pushed). It takes the first gate slot after CF-1, as ruling 0c196f9a orders. Two points need a ruling, and one finding needs a request.\n\nCommits:\n1. d6f9be95: the cap. sqlite.ts keeps the `meta` row `retained-entry-bytes`. `retain` adds a row's size only when its INSERT inserts the row, `forget` subtracts an `entry` row it removes, and both run inside the seal transaction. The constructor fills the row with one scan when it is absent. `#seal` returns the bytes it added, and `#full` applies the stop after `fits` or `capacityFull`. A control delivery, or a result delivery whose clause is not `conflict`, passes the stop. The answer sites in core.ts, delivery.ts and operations.ts add `name: bound` for `scopeBytes`. Seals adopted for turn.ts and core.ts:Scope.\n2. 10837c6e: bounds.ts:66 states both facts of 0c196f9a item c; directory.ts:431 names the create-result admission; \"incarnation\" reads \"genesis\" in the five IDR-1 comments and four lines of session.ts. `export type Incarnation` (scope.ts:19) stays, as a legacy type name.\n3. 5339ccc5: the faults note (notes/implementation/cd-dropped-kind-checks-faults.md) records items 1 to 3 of 1064d0de and the four-kind bound table.\n\nWitnesses in packages/scope/test/capacity-retained-births.test.ts: (a) a scope without the opt-in retains births across a floor, and its next new birth gets `scope-full` with `scopeBytes`; (b) a scope past the cap settles its pending request and refuses a new birth; (c) a scope made before the head gets the true total at its first turn. At 57e893c0 all three fail by assertion: each new birth answers `accepted`. Controls on the head: dropping the result exemption fails (b), and filling the row with 0 fails (c). capacity-confirmation.test.ts changes one case, which asserted that a creation without the opt-in records under `scopeBytes` 0. It now shows that a creation one byte over the cap gets `retry` `scope-full` and writes nothing, and that at the cap the confirmation passes and adds its bytes.\n\nTests, all on battery: Native scope, scope-stories, scope-lending and scope-denial once, 1137 passed, 1 failed, 2 skipped. The failure is a 60 s battery timeout in \"native raw proof residency stays at thirty-two dense frames\"; the file passes alone. It is no evidence, and the gate repeats it on AC. Type checks clean; security seals 12 passed; commit messages 3 checked, 0 flagged.\n\nQuestions:\na. `Store` is an exported interface. `retain` and `retainBirth` now return a number, and `Store` gains `retainedEntryBytes()`. No new export name is added. Does a widened exported interface count as an export under ruling 1064d0de? Proposal: no, because `Store` is the scope's own storage port.\nb. `SqliteStore` writes the `meta` row whenever it opens a store whose row is absent, including a store opened only to read. Proposal: keep it. The write is the first-turn fill of 0c196f9a.\n\nFinding for a new request (0c196f9a item b). Without the opt-in:\n- `definition`: bounded. Only a genesis writes these, at most 16 named plus the scope's own, each at most `definitionBytes`, so at most 4.25 MiB.\n- `rule`: not bounded. The `ruleInputBytes` check runs only under the opt-in, and no floor removes a row.\n- `snapshot`: not bounded. The turn checks the canonical form and no size, and nothing removes a row.\n- `value` (`retained_value`): not bounded over a scope's life. Nothing removes a row.\n\nNoted, with no ruling asked: a diagnosis entry is not exempt from the stop, but today it re-retains only rows the scope holds, so it adds no bytes. Opt-in scopes answer as before, because the new stop runs after `capacityFull`. directory.ts:426 and :436 still say \"incarnation\"; the ruling names only :431."}